<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">On Crimes and Punishments</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1764</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Dei delitti e delle pene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">On Crimes and Punishments is a treatise by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria">Cesare Beccaria</a>, published anonymously in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> in 1764. It argued that punishments should be proportionate, prompt and certain, and condemned torture and capital punishment as cruel and useless. Widely translated and praised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> and other writers, it shaped criminal-law reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and America, including Tuscany's abolition of the death penalty in 1786.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/on-crimes-and-punishments.jpg" alt="On Crimes and Punishments" width="169" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dei_delitti_e_delle_pene_1764.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
